  "summary": "I wrote the whole app in src/main.ts, but I couldn't run, render or type-check it against the engine. `node_modules/@aura3d/` is empty in this sandbox, so `typecheck` reports only TS2307 (cannot find `@aura3d/engine`). I wrote every engine call from the signatures in the docs. The first real build may turn up type errors or runtime differences I couldn't see.\n\n**Camera:** the camera API for moving it at runtime isn't documented anywhere I could read. Instead, the scene uses a fixed camera at the origin. Each frame, nested groups (view-x, view-y, view-t) move and rotate the whole world by the inverse of a virtual orbit camera. The starfield sits inside the rotation groups but outside the translation group. I place the star's point light by hand each frame, so it doesn't depend on lights inheriting group transforms.\n\n**Picking:** I do click-picking myself by projecting each planet to screen space. This assumes the camera's `fov` is vertical. If it's horizontal, hit areas will be slightly off.\n\n**Lighting:** the point-light intensity (40, decay 1) is a guess, and I couldn't judge the exposure.\n\n**Scene:**\n- **Bodies:** a glowing star with halos, plus bloom, and 8 planets with distinct sizes, colours, orbital distances and speeds. Inner planets orbit faster, with periods compressed from real ones.\n- **Look:** planets and moons use generated vertex-colour spheres (bands, continents, craters) and spin about their own axis. Each planet has a thin orbit line.\n- **Moons and ring:** Earth and Jupiter each have a moon, and Saturn has a banded ring.\n- **Background:** 700 stars on a dark background.\n\n**Interaction:**\n- **Focus:** clicking a planet starts an eased 1.5 s move to a close view of it. The camera then follows the planet and stays on its lit side. The info panel shows name, orbital period in Earth days and distance in million km.\n- **Return:** clicking empty space or pressing Escape returns to the overview over 1.5 s and hides the panel.\n- **Overview controls:** drag o",
